{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":62072,"duration_api_ms":62031,"num_turns":16,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"9fed60a5-d6cf-4c8e-9e59-d0333d6f8909","total_cost_usd":0.37648449999999994,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":28218,"cache_read_input_tokens":278244,"output_tokens":2438,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":28218,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":2438,"cacheReadInputTokens":278244,"cacheCreationInputTokens":28218,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.37648449999999994,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"The amendment is technically sound. Code inspection confirms: (1) `temp_condition_min_factor` appears in both the non-negative check (line 913, `>= 0.0`) and the strictly-positive check (line 1005, `> 0.0`), (2) the runtime `check_unit_interval` at line 466-468 accepts `0.0..=1.0`, creating a real inconsistency where preset validation rejects what runtime validation accepts, and (3) the field is only used as a `.clamp(min_factor, 1.0)` floor in `temp_condition_factor` (line 1617/1620), never as a divisor. Zero is a semantically valid value meaning \"factor can drop all the way to zero.\" Removing it from the strictly-positive list is the correct fix.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-6-67e44980","decision":"accept","rationale":"Verified in source: `temp_condition_min_factor` is used exclusively as the lower bound of `.clamp(min_factor, 1.0)` in `temp_condition_factor()` (shrimp.rs:1616-1620) — it is never a divisor. The runtime invariant `check_unit_interval` (invariants.rs:466-468) explicitly accepts `0.0..=1.0`, while the preset strictly-positive check (presets.rs:1005, validated at line 1045 as `> 0.0`) rejects `0.0`. This is a genuine validation inconsistency: a user could set `temp_condition_min_factor = 0.0` through runtime params but the preset system would reject it. Removing it from the strictly-positive list resolves this contradiction. The other fields in that list (e.g., `temp_condition_low_divisor_c`, `temp_condition_high_divisor_c`) are actual divisors where zero would cause division-by-zero, so they correctly remain."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"97f2b61e-fcb3-4f91-a43d-feedb5928bae"}
